ding.8¦␀␀␀␀€–How much of the lookup texture will contribute to the color grading effect.8¦␀␀␀␀€èUse "Gaussian" for a faster but non physical depth of field; "Bokeh" for a more realistic but slower depth of field.8¦␀␀␀␀\The distance at which the blurring will start.8¦␀␀␀␀€‚The distance at which the blurring will reach its maximum radius.8¦␀␀␀␀€´The maximum radius of the gaussian blur. Values above 1 may show under-sampling artifacts.8¦␀␀␀␀€²Use higher quality sampling to reduce flickering and improve the overall blur smoothness.8¦␀␀␀␀FThe distance to the point of focus.8¦␀␀␀␀€æThe ratio of aperture (known as f-stop or f-number). The smaller the value is, the shallower the depth of field is.8¦␀␀␀␀€ÒThe distance between the lens and the film. The larger the value is, the shallower the depth of field is.8¦␀␀␀␀<The number of aperture blades.8¦␀␀␀␀,The curvature of aperture blades. The smaller the value is, the more visible aperture blades are. A value of 1 will make the bokeh perfectly circular.8¦␀␀␀␀VThe rotation of aperture blades in degrees.8¦␀␀␀␀€ÄThe type of grain to use. You can select a preset or provide your own texture by selecting Custom.8¦␀␀␀␀xUse the slider to set the strength of the Film Grain effect.8¦␀␀␀␀€ÚControls the noisiness response curve based on scene luminance. Higher values mean less noise in light areas.8¦␀␀␀␀€¸A tileable texture to use for the grain. The neutral value is 0.5 where no grain is applied.8¦␀␀␀␀0Total distortion amount.8¦␀␀␀␀€žIntensity multiplier on X axis. Set it to 0 to disable distortion on this axis.8¦␀␀␀␀€žIntensity multiplier on Y axis. Set it to 0 to disable distortion on this axis.8¦␀␀␀␀rDistortion center point. 0.5,0.5 is center of the screen.8¦␀␀␀␀€ôControls global screen scaling for the distortion effect. Use this to hide the screen borders when using high "Intensity."8¦␀␀␀␀€êThe motion blur technique to use. If you don't need object motion blur, CameraOnly will result in better performance.8¦␀␀␀␀€ØThe quality of the effect. Lower presets will result in better performance at the expense of visual quality.8¦␀␀␀␀€˜The strength of the motion blur filter. Acts as a multiplier for velocities.8¦␀␀␀␀RSets the maximum length, as a fraction of the screen's full resolution, that the velocity resulting from Camera rotation can have. Lower values will improve performance.8¦␀␀␀␀6Panini projection distance.8¦␀␀␀␀<Panini projection crop to fit.xŒ␀␀␀␀␀;¦␀␀␀␀FlaresxŒ␀␀␀␀␀xŒ␀␀␀␀␀;¦␀␀␀␀Streaks%¨␀␀xŒ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀;¦␀␀␀␀(Chromatic Abberation;¦␀␀8¦␀␀␀␀Shadow Limits␀␀vStart point of the transition between shadows and midtones.8¦␀␀␀␀rEnd point of the transition between shadows and midtones.;¦␀␀8¦␀␀␀␀ Highlight Limits␀␀|Start point of the transition between midtones and highlights.8¦␀␀␀␀xEnd point of the transition between midtones and highlights.8¦␀␀␀␀:The color to use for shadows.8¦␀␀␀␀@The color to use for highlights.8¦␀␀␀␀rBalance between the colors in the highlights and shadows.8¦␀␀␀␀€ˆSelect a tonemapping algorithm to use for the color grading process.xŒ␀␀8¦␀␀␀␀␀␀␀€ÌSpecifies the range reduction mode used when HDR output is enabled and Neutral tonemapping is enabled.8¦␀␀␀␀JUse the ACES preset for HDR displays.8¦␀␀␀␀Specify how much hue to preserve. Values closer to 0 are likely to preserve hue. As values get closer to 1, Unity doesn't correct hue shifts.8¦␀␀␀␀\Enable to use values detected from the output device as paper white. When enabled, output images might differ between SDR and HDR. For best accuracy, set this value manually.8¦␀␀␀␀zThe reference brightness of a paper white surface. This property determines the maximum brightness of UI. The brightness of the scene is scaled relative to this value. The value is in nits.8¦␀␀␀␀4Enable to use the minimum and maximum brightness values detected from the output device. For best accuracy, considering calibrating 